::CW_U01_L03_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "The rain scratched at the roof." Which craft term best describes "scratched"?{
=A. Diction#Correct. "Scratched" is a word choice that gives the rain an uneasy effect.
~B. Theme#Incorrect. Theme is a larger message, not this word choice.
~C. Plot#Incorrect. Plot is event sequence.
~D. Dialogue#Incorrect. No one is speaking.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The rain scratched at the roof." What connotation does "scratched" create?{
~A. Comfort#Incorrect. "Scratched" does not feel soft or comforting.
=B. Unease#Correct. "Scratched" suggests irritation or threat.
~C. Celebration#Incorrect. The word does not suggest joy.
~D. Silence#Incorrect. Scratching implies sound.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "Fine. The plan was perfect. Nothing says perfect like smoke coming from the toaster." What voice is created?{
~A. Formal#Incorrect. The voice is conversational, not formal.
=B. Sarcastic#Correct. The speaker says "perfect" while describing a problem.
~C. Scientific#Incorrect. The speaker is not objective or technical.
~D. Reverent#Incorrect. The voice is not respectful or solemn.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which term means language that appeals to the senses?{
~A. Syntax#Incorrect. Syntax is sentence arrangement.
=B. Imagery#Correct. Imagery appeals to sight, sound, smell, taste, or touch.
~C. Denotation#Incorrect. Denotation is literal meaning.
~D. Plot#Incorrect. Plot is the sequence of events.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Which pair best shows different connotations for a similar denotation?{
~A. table and chair#Incorrect. These are different objects, not connotation differences for a similar meaning.
=B. sprinted and moved#Correct. Both suggest motion, but "sprinted" is faster and more intense.
~C. blue and pencil#Incorrect. These words do not share a similar denotation.
~D. page and window#Incorrect. These are different nouns without the same base meaning.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "The sentence uses short fragments to slow the reader down." Which craft term names sentence arrangement and rhythm?{
~A. Diction#Incorrect. Diction is word choice.
~B. Imagery#Incorrect. Imagery is sensory language.
=C. Syntax#Correct. Syntax refers to sentence structure and arrangement.
~D. Setting#Incorrect. Setting is time and place.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q07::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "No lights. No voices. No way back." What tone does repetition help create?{
=A. Tense#Correct. The repeated "No" intensifies pressure and danger.
~B. Playful#Incorrect. The repetition is severe, not playful.
~C. Casual#Incorrect. The fragments create urgency.
~D. Cheerful#Incorrect. The words suggest threat or fear.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which explanation uses craft vocabulary correctly?{
~A. The story is very good and writerly.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The diction "stumbled" suggests awkward movement and creates an uncertain tone.#Correct. It names diction, explains connotation, and connects to tone.
~C. The paragraph has stuff in it.#Incorrect. This is not precise.
~D. The author used words to make words.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The room was small" and "The room was cramped." How does "cramped" differ from "small"?{
=A. It has the same denotation but a more uncomfortable connotation.#Correct. "Cramped" suggests discomfort while still meaning limited space.
~B. It means the room is larger.#Incorrect. It still suggests small space.
~C. It removes all tone.#Incorrect. It adds tone.
~D. It makes the room sound formal.#Incorrect. The effect is discomfort, not formality.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q10::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The trophy glared from the shelf." What tone is suggested by "glared"?{
=A. Accusing#Correct. "Glared" gives the trophy a harsh, judgmental quality.
~B. Relaxed#Incorrect. "Glared" is not relaxed.
~C. Joyful#Incorrect. The word does not suggest joy.
~D. Neutral#Incorrect. "Glared" is emotionally loaded.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.05] What is denotation?{
~A. The emotional association of a word#Incorrect. That is connotation.
=B. The literal dictionary meaning of a word#Correct. Denotation is the literal meaning.
~C. The rhythm of a sentence#Incorrect. That is syntax.
~D. The main conflict of a story#Incorrect. That is not denotation.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "She clutched the letter" instead of "She held the letter." What does "clutched" suggest?{
=A. A stronger emotional grip#Correct. "Clutched" suggests urgency, fear, or attachment.
~B. A weaker action#Incorrect. It is stronger than "held."
~C. No difference in effect#Incorrect. The connotation changes.
~D. A formal announcement#Incorrect. The word does not suggest formality.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which term names the recognizable pattern of choices that makes writing sound a certain way?{
~A. Draft#Incorrect. A draft is a version of writing.
=B. Style#Correct. Style is the pattern of choices that creates a particular sound or feel.
~C. Paragraph#Incorrect. A paragraph is a unit of writing.
~D. Rubric#Incorrect. A rubric evaluates work.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q14::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "Obviously, the universe had selected my locker as today's disaster zone." Which voice is strongest?{
=A. Sarcastic and dramatic#Correct. The narrator exaggerates the locker problem with comic frustration.
~B. Calm and factual#Incorrect. The sentence is exaggerated, not neutral.
~C. Formal and distant#Incorrect. The voice is informal.
~D. Shy and silent#Incorrect. The voice is expressive.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q15::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Which word has the most positive connotation?{
~A. stubborn#Incorrect. This usually suggests inflexibility.
=B. determined#Correct. "Determined" suggests persistence in a positive way.
~C. pushy#Incorrect. This has a negative connotation.
~D. rigid#Incorrect. This suggests unwillingness to change.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q16::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "The soup whispered steam into the cold kitchen." Which term describes language that gives soup a human action?{
=A. Personification#Correct. Soup is described as whispering, a human action.
~B. Citation#Incorrect. Citation gives source credit.
~C. Summary#Incorrect. Summary restates main points.
~D. Transition#Incorrect. Transition connects ideas.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q17::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The soup whispered steam into the cold kitchen." What tone is most likely?{
=A. Gentle and quiet#Correct. "Whispered" and "steam" create a soft tone.
~B. Violent#Incorrect. The diction is not aggressive.
~C. Silly#Incorrect. The line is quiet, not comic.
~D. Angry#Incorrect. The diction does not suggest anger.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q18::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which feedback best uses academic craft vocabulary?{
=A. The syntax speeds up the action because the sentence uses quick verbs and short phrases.#Correct. It names syntax and explains effect.
~B. It was awesome.#Incorrect. This is vague praise.
~C. I liked the thing near the end.#Incorrect. This lacks precise vocabulary and evidence.
~D. The author wrote writing.#Incorrect. This is not meaningful craft feedback.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q19::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The house waited at the end of the road." What does "waited" suggest?{
=A. The house feels like it has presence or intention.#Correct. The diction gives the house an active, expectant quality.
~B. The house is definitely alive in a literal way.#Incorrect. This is figurative, not literal.
~C. The road is newly built.#Incorrect. The word does not suggest this.
~D. The sentence has no tone.#Incorrect. "Waited" creates mood.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q20::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which sentence best explains imagery?{
~A. Imagery is any sentence with a comma.#Incorrect. Commas do not define imagery.
=B. Imagery is sensory language that helps the reader see, hear, smell, taste, or feel the scene.#Correct. This accurately defines imagery.
~C. Imagery is the title of a story.#Incorrect. A title is not imagery by itself.
~D. Imagery is always dialogue.#Incorrect. Imagery can appear in narration or dialogue.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q21::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The announcement landed in the room, heavy and final." Which word most shapes the serious tone?{
~A. room#Incorrect. This names the place.
=B. final#Correct. "Final" suggests seriousness and no easy change.
~C. the#Incorrect. This article does not create the tone.
~D. in#Incorrect. This preposition does not carry the main tone.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q22::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Which word has the most negative connotation?{
~A. curious#Incorrect. This is usually neutral or positive.
~B. youthful#Incorrect. This is generally positive.
=C. nosy#Correct. "Nosy" suggests intrusive curiosity.
~D. interested#Incorrect. This is neutral or positive.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q23::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Passage: "The paragraph shifts from long reflective sentences to short fragments." Which craft term is being discussed?{
=A. Syntax#Correct. Sentence length and structure are syntax.
~B. Denotation#Incorrect. Denotation is literal word meaning.
~C. Characterization#Incorrect. The question focuses on sentence structure.
~D. Dialogue#Incorrect. The passage does not mention spoken words.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q24::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "I smiled like everything was fine, which was rude of my face, honestly." What tone is created?{
=A. Humorous and self-aware#Correct. The speaker jokes about their own face while revealing discomfort.
~B. Strictly formal#Incorrect. The diction is conversational.
~C. Threatening#Incorrect. The tone is not dangerous.
~D. Detached and scientific#Incorrect. The voice is personal and comic.
}
::CW_U01_L03_Q25::[MLA.CW.WR.05] Which response best meets Lesson 3 mastery expectations?{
~A. The sentence is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. The author used stuff.#Incorrect. This does not name craft.
~C. The line is nice and pretty.#Incorrect. This does not explain effect.
=D. The diction "faded" suggests loss, and that connotation creates a regretful tone.#Correct. This uses precise vocabulary, evidence, and effect.
}
